## 8. Detection Rules (Pure Python, Zero API Calls)

8 rules in `cam/rules/lease_rules.py`, each a pure Python pattern matcher:

| Rule | Signal | What It Detects |
|------|--------|----------------|
| RULE-LS-001 | exception_clause | New "except," "unless," "provided that," "notwithstanding" language |
| RULE-LS-002 | definition_override | Term defined differently in tenant vs template definitions section |
| RULE-LS-003 | qualifier_shift | "shall" → "may", "must" → "should", obligation weakening/strengthening |
| RULE-LS-004 | quantitative_deviation | Different numbers, percentages, dollar amounts, time periods |
| RULE-LS-005 | negation_pattern | Added "not," "no," "neither," limiting language |
| RULE-LS-006 | cross_reference_dependency | Provision references sections that may also differ between documents |
| RULE-LS-007 | omission | Template language present but missing from tenant version |
| RULE-LS-008 | obligation_swap | "Landlord shall" ↔ "Tenant shall" responsibility shifts |

**Design principle:** Rules only flag — they never clear. A rule firing means "this warrants attention." Absence of rule fires does not mean "this is safe" (the evaluators make that determination). This is the CAM one-directional rule principle.

