## 9. Web Application Architecture

### Backend (FastAPI)

**Routes:**

| Method | Path | Purpose |
|--------|------|---------|
| GET | `/` | Serve frontend |
| POST | `/api/auth/verify` | Verify access code |
| POST | `/api/jobs/lease` | Create analysis job (multipart form with file uploads) |
| GET | `/api/jobs/{job_id}` | Poll job status (progress, stage, completion) |
| GET | `/api/jobs/{job_id}/results` | Full results JSON for all tenants |
| GET | `/api/jobs/{job_id}/results/{idx}/annotated` | Download annotated document |
| GET | `/api/jobs/{job_id}/summary` | Generate + download synopsis PDF |
| GET | `/api/provisions` | List 18 default provisions for frontend checklist |
| POST | `/api/jobs/{job_id}/feedback` | Submit agree/disagree/unsure on a finding |
| GET | `/results/{job_id}` | Serve results page (same HTML, JS handles routing) |

**Job lifecycle:**
1. Created → status: `queued`
2. Processing starts in background thread → status: `processing` (with per-tenant progress)
3. Each tenant progresses through 6 stages (progress updates via polling)
4. All tenants done → status: `completed`, `expires_at` set to now + 24 hours
5. Uploaded documents deleted immediately after processing
6. After 24 hours → job expired, results directory cleaned up
7. Periodic cleanup thread runs every 2 minutes

**File handling:**
- Uploads: saved to `uploads/{job_id}/`, deleted after processing completes
- Results: saved to `results/{job_id}/tenant_0/`, `tenant_1/`, etc.
- Supports PDF, DOCX, TXT input (and ZIP containing these)
- TXT files auto-converted to PDF for annotation

### Frontend (Vanilla JS SPA)

Single-page application with 5 states managed by `showState()`:

1. **Gate** — Access code entry
2. **Select** — Analysis type selection (currently only lease comparison)
3. **Upload** — File upload, email, provision selection, custom provisions
4. **Processing** — Real-time progress with stage descriptions and model names
5. **Results** - Full dashboard with Contracts, Contract Summary, Document Comparison, and CAM Audit Trail views

No framework dependencies. No React, Vue, etc. Just vanilla JS + CSS with Inter font from Google Fonts.

### Post-Refactor Frontend Structure

The web app originally concentrated most rendering and workflow logic in a monolithic `static/app.js`. The refactor preserved `app.js` as the top-level orchestrator but moved major shared concerns into focused modules loaded directly by `index.html`:

| Module | Responsibility |
|--------|---------------|
| `app_shared.js` | Shared timing/progress helpers, duration formatting, stage copy, sticky-header offsets, scroll/jump helpers |
| `app_workflow_shared.js` | Workflow clause-set derivation, manual escalation handling, conforming/deviation helpers, contract-resolution helpers |
| `app_audit_shared.js` | Audit Trail formatting helpers, governance/pattern label translation, score-bar rendering |
| `app_docview_shared.js` | Document Comparison TOC/grouping helpers and workflow-row support |
| `app_docview_render_shared.js` | Side-by-side Document Comparison markup builder |
| `app_summary_shared.js` | Contract Summary conforming-card rendering |
| `app_notes_shared.js` | Notes-panel and note-toggle rendering helpers |

This refactor had four architectural goals:
- reduce regression risk from duplicated UI logic living in one file
- unify workflow state across all three contract-detail pages
- separate page-specific rendering from shared formatting/state derivation
- make processing, audit, and comparison behavior easier to evolve independently

### Workflow State Refactor

The refactor also unified clause-state behavior across `Contract Summary`, `Document Comparison`, and `CAM Audit Trail`.

Before the refactor, different pages could derive their own idea of:
- which clauses were active workflow items
- whether a manually escalated conforming clause counted as a deviation
- how contract readiness / remaining issues should be calculated

After the refactor:
- the three pages read from the same workflow-aware clause set
- manually escalated conforming clauses are treated as first-class workflow items
- contract readiness, counts, export sets, and cross-page links use shared helpers
- jump / scroll behavior across the three pages uses shared results-pane helpers instead of duplicated page-specific math

